{
 "id": "DW-08",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-dw-08-f301534feb",
 "endReason": "escalated",
 "customerTurns": 6,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "the door on our built in dishwasher just crashes down when you open it now, used to stay put. Hotpoint I think"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is door hinge / balance / springs (drops open). Does the door physically not close, or does it close and latch but the machine won't start - and does moving the door change anything?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "It closes and latches fine, and the dishwasher washes ok. It's just the door has no resistance when I open it, it drops straight down. Left side still feels a bit tight but the right side has nothing."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Next: On an integrated machine check the furniture-door alignment/balance. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Not sure what you mean by alignment, sorry. The door closes square and latches fine. Do you mean check the hinges or the panel weight? The panel is the same one that was fitted originally."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: door hinge]\nYou should check the hinges, as the lack of resistance on the right side indicates a mechanical failure in the hinge mechanism rather than an issue with the door's weight. The tightness on the left side further suggests that the hinges are not functioning correctly to support the door's movement. The checks point to the door hinge. To find the right part, could you give me the model number? It is usually on the side edge of the door.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The sticker on the door edge is partly peeled, but I can see it starts with LTF. I can't read the rest of it. Is that enough to go on? I also had a look under the plinth with a torch and can see a loose spring hanging down on the right side."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: door hinge]\nYes, seeing a loose spring hanging down on the right side while the left feels tight is sufficient evidence. The issue is caused by a failed or detached door counterbalance spring. Next: With the machine switched off, look for chafed, pinched or broken wiring where the loom passes the hinge/flex point. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I can't really see any wiring from under the plinth, and I can't slide the machine out because the fixing screws are hard to reach and I don't feel confident doing it. Nothing looks obviously wrong from what I can see, but I can't tell about the wiring. Does it matter if I can't check that?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: door hinge]\nYes, it matters because the missing spring on the right side is the direct cause of the door crashing down. You need to replace that specific spring to restore proper resistance and safety when opening the door. With the dishwasher switched off at the socket, look at the latch at the top of the door and the slot it clicks into on the tub, and check the door lines up squarely when you close it. Is the latch or catch broken, was something stopping it (now cleared), or does it all look fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The door closes square and latches fine, nothing looks broken or stuck there as far as I can tell. So is it just the spring that needs replacing? I can't pull the machine out myself, so what do I do next?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: door hinge]\nYes, the loose spring on the right side is causing the door to crash down. Since you cannot move the dishwasher yourself, you should contact a qualified appliance repair technician to replace the broken spring safely. Next: Note whether moving or pressing the door changes anything. Let me know what you find."
  }
 ],
